% Chapter 7 of the literate BitTorrent client

\section{The Client: Central Orchestration}

The [[Client]] struct is the central orchestration point for a BitTorrent download.
It owns all the state needed to manage a torrent: the parsed metadata, piece
tracking, peer connections, and download coordination. While previous chapters
defined individual components in isolation, this chapter brings them together
into a cohesive whole.

\subsection{Design Philosophy}

A BitTorrent client has several responsibilities:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{State ownership}: Hold the torrent metadata, piece manager, and
  connection state in a single place where their lifetimes are coordinated.

\item \textbf{Identity}: Maintain a unique peer ID that identifies us to other
  peers in the swarm.

\item \textbf{Configuration}: Store user preferences like output directory and
  listen port.

\item \textbf{Coordination}: Provide the foundation for the event loop that will
  drive peer communication and piece assembly.
\end{enumerate}

The [[Client]] struct is designed to be the owner of mutable state, passed by
reference to the various subsystems that need to read or modify it. This
centralized ownership simplifies reasoning about lifetimes and mutation.

\subsection{Peer Identification}

Every BitTorrent peer needs a unique 20-byte identifier called a \emph{peer ID}.
The peer ID is sent during the handshake with other peers and reported to
trackers. While the exact format is not mandated by the protocol, convention
is to start with a client identifier followed by random bytes.

We use the Azureus-style convention: [[-LB0001-]] followed by 12 random bytes.
Here [[LB]] stands for ``Literate BitTorrent'' and [[0001]] is our version
number. The hyphen prefix and suffix are traditional.

<<peer id generation>>=
use rand::Rng;

/// Generates a unique 20-byte peer ID in Azureus style.
///
/// Format: `-LB0001-` followed by 12 random alphanumeric bytes.
///
/// - `LB` = Literate BitTorrent (our client identifier)
/// - `0001` = version 0.0.0.1
/// - `-` prefix and suffix follow the Azureus convention
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::client::generate_peer_id;
///
/// let peer_id = generate_peer_id();
/// assert_eq!(peer_id.len(), 20);
/// assert!(peer_id.starts_with(b"-LB0001-"));
/// ```
pub fn generate_peer_id() -> [u8; 20] {
    let mut peer_id = [0u8; 20];

    // Client identifier prefix: -LB0001-
    peer_id[..8].copy_from_slice(b"-LB0001-");

    // Fill remaining 12 bytes with random alphanumeric characters
    let mut rng = rand::thread_rng();
    const CHARSET: &[u8] = b"0123456789ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz";

    for byte in &mut peer_id[8..] {
        *byte = CHARSET[rng.gen_range(0..CHARSET.len())];
    }

    peer_id
}
@

Using alphanumeric characters in the random portion ensures the peer ID is
printable, which aids debugging when examining protocol traces.

\subsection{Request Queue Backpressure}

When downloading from a peer, BitTorrent clients pipeline multiple block
requests to maximize throughput. Without pipelining, each request would incur
a full round-trip latency before the next could be sent. With pipelining, the
network stays saturated as responses flow back continuously.

However, unbounded pipelining creates problems:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Memory pressure}: Each outstanding request consumes memory both
  locally (tracking state) and on the peer (queued response data).

\item \textbf{Peer overwhelm}: Peers may throttle or disconnect clients that
  send too many requests too fast.

\item \textbf{Timeout complexity}: Too many outstanding requests makes timeout
  detection and retry logic more complex.

\item \textbf{Fairness}: A peer that accepts unlimited requests may starve
  other peers waiting for upload bandwidth.
\end{enumerate}

The solution is a \emph{bounded request queue} per peer. By limiting the queue
to a fixed size (typically 10--16 requests), we achieve good pipelining while
providing natural backpressure. When the queue is full, the scheduler must
wait for responses before sending more requests to that peer.

The [[PeerRequestQueue]] struct implements this bounded queue. Each request
is wrapped in [[TimestampedRequest]] to record when it was sent, enabling
timeout detection. The queue operates in FIFO order for fairness.

\subsubsection{Request Timeout Detection}

Peers sometimes become unresponsive: they may crash, lose network connectivity,
or simply deprioritize our requests. Without timeout detection, we would wait
indefinitely for responses that never come, stalling the download.

The PRD specifies a 30-second timeout ([[REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECS]]). Requests
pending longer than this are considered timed out and should be re-requested
from another peer. The [[check_timeouts]] method provides convenient access
to this list.

\textbf{Design choice: detection vs removal.} Timed-out requests are \emph{not}
automatically removed from the queue. This separation is intentional:

\begin{enumerate}
\item The caller first identifies timed-out requests via [[check_timeouts]].
\item The caller re-requests those blocks from another peer.
\item Only after the new request is queued does the caller remove the old one.
\end{enumerate}

This ordering prevents a race condition where a block is neither in-flight nor
queued for re-request. By keeping the timed-out request in the queue until a
replacement is scheduled, we maintain the invariant that every needed block is
tracked somewhere.

<<peer request queue>>=
use std::collections::VecDeque;
use std::time::{Duration, Instant};

use crate::pieces::BlockRequest;

/// Default maximum pending requests per peer.
///
/// Typically 10-16 for good pipelining without overwhelming peers.
/// This value represents a balance between throughput (more requests
/// keep the pipe full) and resource usage (each request consumes memory
/// and peer attention).
///
/// The BitTorrent Economics Paper suggests 10 requests as a reasonable
/// default. Some clients use up to 16 for high-latency connections.
pub const DEFAULT_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_PEER: usize = 16;

/// Default timeout for block requests in seconds.
///
/// Requests pending longer than this should be cancelled and re-requested
/// from another peer. The 30-second value comes from the PRD specification
/// and represents a balance between:
/// - Giving slow peers enough time to respond
/// - Not waiting too long on unresponsive peers
///
/// See [`PeerRequestQueue::check_timeouts`] for the convenience method
/// that uses this default.
pub const REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECS: u64 = 30;

/// A request with timestamp for timeout detection.
///
/// Wraps a [`BlockRequest`] with the instant it was sent. This enables:
/// - Timeout detection by comparing `sent_at` to current time
/// - Latency measurement for peer performance tracking
/// - FIFO ordering verification in debugging
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct TimestampedRequest {
    /// The underlying block request.
    pub request: BlockRequest,
    /// When this request was added to the queue.
    pub sent_at: Instant,
}

/// Bounded queue of in-flight block requests for a single peer.
///
/// Provides backpressure by rejecting requests when at capacity.
/// Tracks request timestamps for timeout detection.
///
/// # Design
///
/// The queue uses [`VecDeque`] for O(1) push/pop at both ends. While
/// `remove` is O(n), this operation is only needed when a block response
/// arrives out of order, which is relatively rare. The typical flow is:
/// FIFO---push requests at the back, receive responses for front requests.
///
/// # Backpressure
///
/// When `is_full()` returns true, the caller should stop requesting from
/// this peer until space becomes available. This creates natural flow
/// control: fast peers drain their queues quickly and receive more work,
/// while slow peers remain at capacity.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::client::{PeerRequestQueue, DEFAULT_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_PEER};
/// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::BlockRequest;
///
/// let mut queue = PeerRequestQueue::new(2);
/// let peer_id = *b"-LB0001-test12345678";
///
/// // Add requests until full
/// assert!(queue.push(BlockRequest::new(0, 0, 16384, peer_id)));
/// assert!(queue.push(BlockRequest::new(0, 16384, 16384, peer_id)));
/// assert!(queue.is_full());
///
/// // Further pushes are rejected
/// assert!(!queue.push(BlockRequest::new(1, 0, 16384, peer_id)));
///
/// // Remove a request to make room
/// let removed = queue.remove(0, 0);
/// assert!(removed.is_some());
/// assert!(!queue.is_full());
/// ```
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct PeerRequestQueue {
    /// Pending requests in FIFO order.
    requests: VecDeque<TimestampedRequest>,
    /// Maximum queue size.
    max_size: usize,
}

impl PeerRequestQueue {
    /// Creates a new queue with the specified maximum size.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `max_size` - Maximum number of concurrent requests to allow
    ///
    /// # Panics
    ///
    /// Does not panic, but a `max_size` of 0 means all pushes will fail.
    pub fn new(max_size: usize) -> Self {
        Self {
            requests: VecDeque::with_capacity(max_size),
            max_size,
        }
    }

    /// Creates a new queue with default max size (16 requests).
    ///
    /// This is the recommended constructor for most use cases.
    pub fn with_default_size() -> Self {
        Self::new(DEFAULT_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_PEER)
    }

    /// Adds a request to the queue if not full.
    ///
    /// Returns `true` if the request was added, `false` if the queue
    /// is at capacity and the request was rejected.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `request` - The block request to enqueue
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::PeerRequestQueue;
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::BlockRequest;
    ///
    /// let mut queue = PeerRequestQueue::new(1);
    /// let peer_id = *b"-LB0001-test12345678";
    ///
    /// assert!(queue.push(BlockRequest::new(0, 0, 16384, peer_id)));
    /// assert!(!queue.push(BlockRequest::new(0, 16384, 16384, peer_id))); // rejected
    /// ```
    pub fn push(&mut self, request: BlockRequest) -> bool {
        if self.requests.len() >= self.max_size {
            return false;
        }
        self.requests.push_back(TimestampedRequest {
            request,
            sent_at: Instant::now(),
        });
        true
    }

    /// Removes a request matching the given piece_index and offset.
    ///
    /// Returns the request if found, `None` otherwise. This is used when
    /// a block response arrives and we need to remove the corresponding
    /// request from the queue.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `piece_index` - The piece index to match
    /// * `offset` - The byte offset within the piece to match
    ///
    /// # Performance
    ///
    /// This is O(n) in the queue length. For the typical queue size of
    /// 10-16, this is negligible. If responses arrive in FIFO order,
    /// the matching request is at the front and removal is O(1).
    pub fn remove(&mut self, piece_index: u32, offset: u32) -> Option<TimestampedRequest> {
        let pos = self.requests.iter().position(|r| {
            r.request.piece_index == piece_index && r.request.offset == offset
        })?;
        self.requests.remove(pos)
    }

    /// Returns true if the queue is at capacity.
    ///
    /// When this returns `true`, further `push` calls will return `false`.
    /// The caller should wait for responses or timeouts before sending
    /// more requests to this peer.
    #[inline]
    pub fn is_full(&self) -> bool {
        self.requests.len() >= self.max_size
    }

    /// Returns the number of pending requests.
    #[inline]
    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {
        self.requests.len()
    }

    /// Returns true if the queue is empty.
    #[inline]
    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
        self.requests.is_empty()
    }

    /// Returns an iterator over pending requests (for timeout checking).
    ///
    /// The iterator yields requests in FIFO order. This is useful for
    /// checking timeouts: older requests appear first, so timeout
    /// checking can stop early once a non-timed-out request is found.
    pub fn iter(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = &TimestampedRequest> {
        self.requests.iter()
    }

    /// Returns requests that have been pending longer than the given duration.
    ///
    /// This collects all timed-out requests into a vector. The caller
    /// can then decide what to do: cancel and re-request from another
    /// peer, mark the peer as slow, or disconnect entirely.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `timeout` - Duration after which a request is considered timed out
    pub fn timed_out(&self, timeout: Duration) -> Vec<TimestampedRequest> {
        self.requests
            .iter()
            .filter(|r| r.sent_at.elapsed() > timeout)
            .cloned()
            .collect()
    }

    /// Returns requests that have timed out using the default timeout.
    ///
    /// This is a convenience wrapper around [`timed_out`](Self::timed_out)
    /// using [`REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECS`] (30 seconds) as the threshold.
    ///
    /// # Design Note
    ///
    /// Timed-out requests are *not* removed from the queue. This is
    /// intentional: the caller should first re-request the block from
    /// another peer, then remove the timed-out request. This prevents
    /// a race where the block is neither in-flight nor re-requested.
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use std::time::Instant;
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::PeerRequestQueue;
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::BlockRequest;
    ///
    /// let mut queue = PeerRequestQueue::new(10);
    /// queue.push(BlockRequest {
    ///     piece_index: 0,
    ///     offset: 0,
    ///     length: 16384,
    ///     requested_at: Instant::now(),
    ///     peer: [0u8; 20],
    /// });
    ///
    /// // Immediately after push, nothing should be timed out
    /// let timed_out = queue.check_timeouts();
    /// assert!(timed_out.is_empty());
    /// ```
    pub fn check_timeouts(&self) -> Vec<TimestampedRequest> {
        self.timed_out(Duration::from_secs(REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECS))
    }

    /// Returns requests that have timed out using a custom timeout.
    ///
    /// This is useful for:
    /// - Testing with shorter timeouts
    /// - Applying stricter timeouts to slow peers
    /// - Implementing adaptive timeout strategies
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `timeout` - Duration after which a request is considered timed out
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use std::time::{Duration, Instant};
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::PeerRequestQueue;
    /// use literate_bittorrent::pieces::BlockRequest;
    ///
    /// let mut queue = PeerRequestQueue::new(10);
    /// queue.push(BlockRequest {
    ///     piece_index: 0,
    ///     offset: 0,
    ///     length: 16384,
    ///     requested_at: Instant::now(),
    ///     peer: [0u8; 20],
    /// });
    ///
    /// // With zero duration, everything is timed out
    /// let timed_out = queue.check_timeouts_with(Duration::ZERO);
    /// assert!(!timed_out.is_empty());
    /// ```
    pub fn check_timeouts_with(&self, timeout: Duration) -> Vec<TimestampedRequest> {
        self.timed_out(timeout)
    }

    /// Clears all pending requests, returning them.
    ///
    /// This is used when a peer chokes us---all outstanding requests
    /// are effectively cancelled and should be re-queued for other peers.
    pub fn drain(&mut self) -> impl Iterator<Item = TimestampedRequest> + '_ {
        self.requests.drain(..)
    }
}
@

\subsection{Client State}

The [[Client]] struct holds all state for a single torrent download. In the
future, a multi-torrent client could maintain multiple [[Client]] instances,
one per torrent.

<<client struct>>=
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::net::SocketAddr;

use crate::metainfo::Metainfo;
use crate::pieces::PieceManager;

/// Central orchestration point for a BitTorrent download.
///
/// The `Client` owns all state needed to manage a torrent download:
/// - Torrent metadata from the .torrent file
/// - Piece tracking for download progress and verification
/// - Our peer identity for protocol handshakes
/// - Configuration like output directory and listen port
///
/// # Design Notes
///
/// The `Client` is designed as a single owner of mutable state. Subsystems
/// like peer connections receive references to the client rather than owning
/// their own copies of state. This simplifies lifetime management and makes
/// state changes explicit.
///
/// # Async Runtime
///
/// BitTorrent clients are inherently concurrent: we communicate with many
/// peers simultaneously, each in their own connection. The `runtime_handle`
/// field stores a handle to the Tokio runtime, allowing the client to spawn
/// tasks for peer connections.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```no_run
/// use std::path::PathBuf;
/// use literate_bittorrent::client::Client;
/// use literate_bittorrent::metainfo;
///
/// // Parse the torrent file
/// let torrent_data = std::fs::read("example.torrent").unwrap();
/// let metainfo = metainfo::parse(&torrent_data).unwrap();
///
/// // Create client with default configuration
/// let client = Client::new(
///     metainfo,
///     PathBuf::from("./downloads"),
///     6881,
/// ).unwrap();
///
/// println!("Downloading: {}", client.name());
/// println!("Info hash: {:?}", client.info_hash());
/// ```
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct Client {
    /// Parsed torrent metadata.
    ///
    /// Contains tracker URL, file information, piece hashes, and other
    /// data from the .torrent file.
    metainfo: Metainfo,

    /// The 20-byte SHA1 hash identifying this torrent.
    ///
    /// This is cached from `metainfo.info_hash` for convenience, as it's
    /// needed frequently for handshakes and tracker announces.
    info_hash: [u8; 20],

    /// Our unique 20-byte peer identifier.
    ///
    /// Generated once at client creation and used for all peer handshakes
    /// and tracker announces.
    peer_id: [u8; 20],

    /// Tracks download state for each piece.
    ///
    /// The piece manager owns the bitfield of which pieces we have,
    /// tracks in-progress downloads, and verifies completed pieces.
    piece_manager: PieceManager,

    /// Directory where downloaded files will be saved.
    output_dir: PathBuf,

    /// TCP port for incoming peer connections.
    ///
    /// This port is announced to the tracker so other peers can connect
    /// to us. Standard BitTorrent ports are 6881-6889.
    port: u16,

    /// Currently connected peers, keyed by their socket address.
    ///
    /// This map tracks all active peer connections. The value will be
    /// expanded to hold peer-specific state (choking, interested, bitfield)
    /// in future chapters.
    active_peers: HashMap<SocketAddr, PeerState>,

    /// Handle to the Tokio async runtime.
    ///
    /// Used to spawn tasks for peer connections. Stored here so all
    /// async operations use the same runtime.
    runtime_handle: tokio::runtime::Handle,

    /// Time when the download started.
    ///
    /// Used to calculate total download time and average speed when
    /// the download completes. Set to the current instant when the
    /// client is created.
    start_time: std::time::Instant,

    /// Flag indicating the download is complete.
    ///
    /// Set to `true` when all pieces have been verified. Once set,
    /// no further piece requests should be made. This flag is checked
    /// by the request scheduler to prevent requesting pieces from a
    /// completed download.
    download_complete: bool,

    /// Flag indicating the completed event has been announced.
    ///
    /// The `event=completed` announce should be sent exactly once when
    /// the download finishes. This flag prevents duplicate announces
    /// if `on_piece_verified` is called multiple times after completion.
    completed_announced: bool,
}

/// Per-peer connection state tracking the 4-bit choking/interest state machine.
///
/// The BitTorrent protocol uses a symmetric state machine between peers. Each
/// side maintains two bits about itself (am I choking them? am I interested?)
/// and infers two bits about the remote peer (are they choking me? are they
/// interested?). This creates a 4-bit state that determines data flow:
///
/// ```text
///   am_choking      - We are refusing to upload to them (default: true)
///   am_interested   - We want pieces they have (default: false)
///   peer_choking    - They are refusing to upload to us (default: true)
///   peer_interested - They want pieces we have (default: false)
/// ```
///
/// # Data Flow Conditions
///
/// Data transfer can only occur when certain conditions are met:
///
/// - **We can request from them** when: `!peer_choking && am_interested`
///   (they're willing to send, and we want data)
/// - **They can request from us** when: `!am_choking && peer_interested`
///   (we're willing to send, and they want data)
///
/// # State Transitions
///
/// State changes happen via protocol messages:
/// - `choke` / `unchoke` messages change the choking state
/// - `interested` / `not interested` messages change the interest state
///
/// When a peer chokes us, all pending requests to that peer should be
/// considered cancelled and may need to be re-requested from other peers.
///
/// # Bitfield Tracking
///
/// The `bitfield` tracks which pieces the remote peer has. This is initialized
/// from the peer's `bitfield` message (sent after handshake) and updated by
/// `have` messages as they complete pieces.
///
/// # Request Pipelining
///
/// `pending_requests` tracks in-flight block requests to this peer. BitTorrent
/// allows pipelining multiple requests (typically 10-16) to maximize throughput.
/// Each request includes a timestamp for timeout detection.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct PeerState {
    /// True if we are choking this peer (refusing to upload).
    ///
    /// Default is `true` because we start by choking all peers until we
    /// decide to unchoke them based on our upload strategy.
    pub am_choking: bool,

    /// True if we are interested in this peer's pieces.
    ///
    /// We become interested when the peer has pieces we need. This is
    /// updated when we receive their bitfield or have messages.
    pub am_interested: bool,

    /// True if the peer is choking us (refusing to upload).
    ///
    /// Default is `true` because peers start by choking us. We must wait
    /// for an `unchoke` message before we can request pieces.
    pub peer_choking: bool,

    /// True if the peer is interested in our pieces.
    ///
    /// Updated when we receive `interested` or `not interested` messages.
    pub peer_interested: bool,

    /// Bitfield of pieces this peer has.
    ///
    /// Each bit represents one piece: bit i is set if the peer has piece i.
    /// Bits are packed into bytes, with bit 0 of byte 0 representing piece 0.
    /// The size is `ceil(num_pieces / 8)` bytes.
    pub bitfield: Vec<u8>,

    /// Queue of pending block requests sent to this peer.
    ///
    /// BitTorrent allows pipelining multiple requests to maximize throughput.
    /// Each request includes timing information for timeout detection. When
    /// the peer chokes us, these requests are effectively cancelled.
    pub pending_requests: VecDeque<BlockRequest>,
}

impl PeerState {
    /// Creates a new PeerState with protocol-default values.
    ///
    /// The initial state reflects BitTorrent's connection semantics:
    /// - Both sides start choking each other (no data transfer)
    /// - Neither side is interested yet
    /// - The bitfield is empty (sized for the torrent)
    /// - No pending requests
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `num_pieces` - Total number of pieces in the torrent
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::PeerState;
    ///
    /// let state = PeerState::new(100); // 100-piece torrent
    /// assert!(state.am_choking);       // We choke them initially
    /// assert!(!state.am_interested);   // Not interested until we see their pieces
    /// assert!(state.peer_choking);     // They choke us initially
    /// assert!(!state.peer_interested);
    /// ```
    pub fn new(num_pieces: u32) -> Self {
        // Calculate bitfield size: ceil(num_pieces / 8)
        let bitfield_len = ((num_pieces + 7) / 8) as usize;

        PeerState {
            am_choking: true,       // We choke them by default
            am_interested: false,   // Not interested until we see their pieces
            peer_choking: true,     // Assume they're choking us until told otherwise
            peer_interested: false, // Assume they're not interested
            bitfield: vec![0u8; bitfield_len],
            pending_requests: VecDeque::new(),
        }
    }

    /// Returns true if we can send requests to this peer.
    ///
    /// We can only request pieces when:
    /// 1. The peer is NOT choking us (willing to send data)
    /// 2. We ARE interested in their pieces
    ///
    /// Both conditions must be true. If we're not interested, requesting
    /// would be pointless. If they're choking us, they'll ignore our requests.
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::PeerState;
    ///
    /// let mut state = PeerState::new(100);
    ///
    /// // Initially can't request: we're not interested and they're choking
    /// assert!(!state.can_request());
    ///
    /// // Become interested and get unchoked
    /// state.am_interested = true;
    /// state.peer_choking = false;
    /// assert!(state.can_request());
    ///
    /// // If they choke us again, can't request
    /// state.peer_choking = true;
    /// assert!(!state.can_request());
    /// ```
    #[inline]
    pub fn can_request(&self) -> bool {
        !self.peer_choking && self.am_interested
    }

    /// Returns true if the peer has the specified piece.
    ///
    /// Checks the bitfield to determine if the peer has announced ownership
    /// of the given piece (either via bitfield message or have message).
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `index` - The piece index to check
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// `true` if the peer has the piece, `false` otherwise. Returns `false`
    /// for out-of-bounds indices (defensive behavior).
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::PeerState;
    ///
    /// let mut state = PeerState::new(100);
    ///
    /// // Initially peer has no pieces
    /// assert!(!state.has_piece(0));
    /// assert!(!state.has_piece(42));
    ///
    /// // Simulate receiving have(42)
    /// let byte_idx = 42 / 8;
    /// let bit_idx = 7 - (42 % 8); // MSB first
    /// state.bitfield[byte_idx] |= 1 << bit_idx;
    ///
    /// assert!(state.has_piece(42));
    /// assert!(!state.has_piece(41)); // Other pieces still missing
    /// ```
    #[inline]
    pub fn has_piece(&self, index: u32) -> bool {
        let byte_idx = (index / 8) as usize;
        let bit_idx = 7 - (index % 8); // MSB first (BitTorrent convention)

        if byte_idx >= self.bitfield.len() {
            return false; // Out of bounds, defensive
        }

        (self.bitfield[byte_idx] & (1 << bit_idx)) != 0
    }

    /// Sets the bit for a piece in the bitfield (peer has this piece).
    ///
    /// Called when processing a `have` message from the peer.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `index` - The piece index to mark as available
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// `true` if the bit was set, `false` if index was out of bounds.
    pub fn set_piece(&mut self, index: u32) -> bool {
        let byte_idx = (index / 8) as usize;
        let bit_idx = 7 - (index % 8); // MSB first

        if byte_idx >= self.bitfield.len() {
            return false;
        }

        self.bitfield[byte_idx] |= 1 << bit_idx;
        true
    }

    /// Returns the number of pieces this peer has.
    ///
    /// Counts all set bits in the bitfield. Useful for determining
    /// if a peer is a seeder (has all pieces) or assessing peer value.
    pub fn piece_count(&self) -> u32 {
        self.bitfield
            .iter()
            .map(|byte| byte.count_ones())
            .sum()
    }

    /// Returns true if this peer is a seeder (has all pieces).
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `num_pieces` - Total number of pieces in the torrent
    pub fn is_seeder(&self, num_pieces: u32) -> bool {
        self.piece_count() == num_pieces
    }

    // ========================================================================
    // Message Handlers
    // ========================================================================
    //
    // These methods update PeerState in response to protocol messages received
    // from the remote peer. Each handler corresponds to a specific message type
    // and updates the relevant state fields.

    /// Handle a `choke` message from the peer.
    ///
    /// When the peer chokes us, they are indicating they will not respond to
    /// our block requests. We must:
    /// 1. Set `peer_choking` to true
    /// 2. Clear all pending requests (the peer has effectively cancelled them)
    ///
    /// The pending requests should be re-queued for other peers or re-requested
    /// when this peer unchokes us. The caller is responsible for this recovery.
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::PeerState;
    ///
    /// let mut state = PeerState::new(100);
    /// state.peer_choking = false; // Simulate having been unchoked
    ///
    /// // Simulate some pending requests
    /// // (In production, these would be BlockRequest structs)
    ///
    /// state.handle_choke();
    /// assert!(state.peer_choking);
    /// assert!(state.pending_requests.is_empty());
    /// ```
    pub fn handle_choke(&mut self) {
        self.peer_choking = true;
        self.pending_requests.clear();
    }

    /// Handle an `unchoke` message from the peer.
    ///
    /// When the peer unchokes us, they are indicating they will respond to
    /// our block requests. This sets `peer_choking` to false, allowing
    /// `can_request()` to return true (if we are also interested).
    ///
    /// After receiving an unchoke, the client can start sending block requests
    /// to this peer. The request scheduler should prioritize this peer.
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::PeerState;
    ///
    /// let mut state = PeerState::new(100);
    /// assert!(state.peer_choking); // Initially choked
    ///
    /// state.handle_unchoke();
    /// assert!(!state.peer_choking);
    /// ```
    pub fn handle_unchoke(&mut self) {
        self.peer_choking = false;
    }

    /// Handle an `interested` message from the peer.
    ///
    /// When the peer sends `interested`, they are indicating they want pieces
    /// that we have. This sets `peer_interested` to true.
    ///
    /// In a seeding implementation, this would signal that we should consider
    /// unchoking this peer to upload to them. Since this client does not
    /// implement seeding (per non-goals), we track this state for protocol
    /// completeness but don't act on it.
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::PeerState;
    ///
    /// let mut state = PeerState::new(100);
    /// assert!(!state.peer_interested); // Initially not interested
    ///
    /// state.handle_interested();
    /// assert!(state.peer_interested);
    /// ```
    pub fn handle_interested(&mut self) {
        self.peer_interested = true;
    }

    /// Handle a `not interested` message from the peer.
    ///
    /// When the peer sends `not interested`, they are indicating they no longer
    /// want pieces that we have. This sets `peer_interested` to false.
    ///
    /// In a seeding implementation, this would signal that we might want to
    /// choke this peer to free up upload slots for interested peers.
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::PeerState;
    ///
    /// let mut state = PeerState::new(100);
    /// state.peer_interested = true; // Simulate having received interested
    ///
    /// state.handle_not_interested();
    /// assert!(!state.peer_interested);
    /// ```
    pub fn handle_not_interested(&mut self) {
        self.peer_interested = false;
    }

    /// Handle a `have` message from the peer.
    ///
    /// The `have` message indicates the peer has completed downloading and
    /// verifying a piece. We update our bitfield to reflect this.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `index` - The piece index the peer now has
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `true` if the index was valid and the bit was set
    /// * `false` if the index was out of bounds
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::PeerState;
    ///
    /// let mut state = PeerState::new(100);
    /// assert!(!state.has_piece(42));
    ///
    /// assert!(state.handle_have(42)); // Valid index
    /// assert!(state.has_piece(42));
    ///
    /// assert!(!state.handle_have(200)); // Invalid index (out of bounds)
    /// ```
    pub fn handle_have(&mut self, index: u32) -> bool {
        // Delegate to set_piece which already handles bounds checking
        self.set_piece(index)
    }

    /// Handle a `bitfield` message from the peer.
    ///
    /// The `bitfield` message is sent immediately after the handshake to indicate
    /// which pieces the peer already has. We replace our empty bitfield with
    /// the received one, after validating the length.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `bits` - The bitfield bytes from the peer
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `Ok(())` if the bitfield was valid and accepted
    /// * `Err(PeerError::BitfieldWrongLength)` if the length doesn't match
    ///
    /// # Errors
    ///
    /// Returns an error if `bits.len()` does not equal `self.bitfield.len()`.
    /// This indicates a protocol violation: the peer's bitfield must have
    /// exactly `ceil(num_pieces / 8)` bytes.
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::PeerState;
    ///
    /// let mut state = PeerState::new(16); // 16 pieces = 2 bytes
    ///
    /// // Valid bitfield: peer has pieces 0, 8, and 15
    /// let bits = vec![0b1000_0000, 0b1000_0001]; // 2 bytes
    /// assert!(state.handle_bitfield(&bits).is_ok());
    /// assert!(state.has_piece(0));
    /// assert!(state.has_piece(8));
    /// assert!(state.has_piece(15));
    ///
    /// // Invalid bitfield: wrong length
    /// let bad_bits = vec![0xFF, 0xFF, 0xFF]; // 3 bytes, expected 2
    /// let mut state2 = PeerState::new(16);
    /// assert!(state2.handle_bitfield(&bad_bits).is_err());
    /// ```
    pub fn handle_bitfield(&mut self, bits: &[u8]) -> Result<(), crate::messages::PeerError> {
        if bits.len() != self.bitfield.len() {
            return Err(crate::messages::PeerError::BitfieldWrongLength {
                expected: self.bitfield.len(),
                actual: bits.len(),
            });
        }
        self.bitfield.copy_from_slice(bits);
        Ok(())
    }
}

impl Default for PeerState {
    /// Creates a PeerState with a zero-length bitfield.
    ///
    /// This is primarily useful for testing. In production, use `PeerState::new()`
    /// with the actual number of pieces.
    fn default() -> Self {
        PeerState {
            am_choking: true,
            am_interested: false,
            peer_choking: true,
            peer_interested: false,
            bitfield: Vec::new(),
            pending_requests: VecDeque::new(),
        }
    }
}
