\subsection{Peer Connection Manager}

The [[PeerConnectionManager]] orchestrates the pool of active peer connections.
In a BitTorrent swarm, we typically want to maintain connections to many peers
simultaneously---the PRD specifies 30 concurrent connections by default. This
presents several challenges:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Connection lifecycle}: Peers come and go. We need to track which
  peers we're connected to and replace disconnected ones with fresh addresses
  from our available pool.

\item \textbf{Address management}: The tracker returns a list of peer addresses,
  but we can't connect to all of them at once. We queue addresses and draw from
  the pool as slots become available.

\item \textbf{Duplicate prevention}: The same peer address might appear in
  multiple tracker responses. We must not create duplicate connections.

\item \textbf{State isolation}: Each peer has its own 4-bit choke/interest
  state machine and bitfield. The connection manager owns this per-peer state.
\end{enumerate}

The [[PeerConnectionManager]] addresses these concerns by maintaining two
collections: a map of active connections keyed by socket address, and a queue
of available addresses not yet connected.

\subsubsection{Design Decisions}

We use [[VecDeque]] for available peers rather than [[HashSet]] for several
reasons:

\begin{itemize}
\item FIFO ordering: Connect to peers in the order received from tracker
\item Efficient front/back operations: [[push\_back]] and [[pop\_front]] are O(1)
\item Simpler implementation: No need for random selection or ordering logic
\end{itemize}

The connection map uses [[SocketAddr]] as the key because a peer is uniquely
identified by their IP:port combination. Using [[SocketAddr]] also makes it
trivial to detect duplicate connection attempts.

\subsubsection{Constants and Connection Utilities}

Connection timeouts are essential for robustness in peer-to-peer networks.
Without explicit timeouts, the operating system's default TCP connection
timeout (often 2+ minutes on Linux) would cause the client to block indefinitely
when attempting to connect to offline or firewalled peers.

The [[connect\_with\_timeout]] function wraps [[TcpStream::connect]] with a
10-second timeout as specified in the PRD. This timeout balances two concerns:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Responsiveness}: We don't want to wait too long for unresponsive
  peers when there are many other peers available in the swarm.
\item \textbf{Reliability}: We don't want to give up too quickly on peers that
  may simply be on high-latency networks. Ten seconds is sufficient for even
  intercontinental connections.
\end{enumerate}

The function returns [[PeerError::Timeout]] when the timeout elapses and
[[PeerError::IoError]] for immediate failures like connection refused.

\subsubsection{Handshake Exchange}

After establishing a TCP connection, BitTorrent peers must exchange handshakes
before any other protocol messages can be sent. The handshake serves two critical
purposes:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Protocol identification}: Each handshake contains the protocol
  string ``BitTorrent protocol'' (prefixed by its length, 19). This allows early
  detection of non-BitTorrent connections.

\item \textbf{Torrent identification}: Each handshake contains a 20-byte info\_hash
  identifying the torrent. Both peers must have matching info\_hashes, or they're
  talking about different torrents.
\end{enumerate}

The [[perform\_handshake]] function implements this exchange as a send-then-receive
sequence:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Send our 68-byte handshake containing our info\_hash and peer\_id.
\item Read exactly 68 bytes from the peer.
\item Parse the received bytes and validate protocol correctness.
\item Verify the info\_hash matches what we expect.
\end{enumerate}

Like TCP connection, the handshake exchange requires a timeout. A peer might accept
our connection but never send their handshake---perhaps due to being overloaded,
buggy, or malicious. Without a timeout, we would block indefinitely waiting for
bytes that never arrive. The [[HANDSHAKE\_TIMEOUT\_SECS]] constant (10 seconds)
provides the same balance of responsiveness and reliability as the connection
timeout.

The timeout wraps the entire exchange, not each individual operation. This is
simpler and more flexible: a peer that takes 8 seconds to respond to our write
but immediately sends their handshake is acceptable; a peer that takes 6 seconds
for each operation (12 seconds total) is not.

Upon successful validation, we return the peer's [[Handshake]] struct. The caller
can extract the peer\_id to track this connection and log communication. If
validation fails, we return [[PeerError::HandshakeFailed]] with details about
what went wrong.

