\subsection{Framed Message Writing}

While reading messages requires careful handling of length prefixes and partial
reads, writing messages is simpler but equally important. The [[write_message]]
function serializes a message and writes it atomically to the TCP stream.

\subsubsection{Keep-Alive Interval}

The BitTorrent protocol requires peers to send keep-alive messages every two
minutes (120 seconds) to prevent connection timeouts. A keep-alive is simply
a zero-length message (four zero bytes). If no messages have been sent for
120 seconds, we must send a keep-alive to signal that the connection is still
active.

<<framed message writing>>=
/// Keep-alive interval in seconds. Connection is considered idle after this.
///
/// Per BEP 3, peers should send keep-alives every two minutes to prevent
/// connection timeouts. Idle connections without keep-alives may be closed
/// by the peer, network equipment, or firewalls implementing connection
/// tracking.
pub const KEEP_ALIVE_INTERVAL_SECS: u64 = 120;

/// Writes a message to the TCP stream.
///
/// Serializes the message using `Message::to_bytes()` which includes
/// the 4-byte length prefix. Uses write_all for atomic writes.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `stream` - A mutable reference to the TCP connection
/// * `msg` - The message to send
///
/// # Returns
///
/// * `Ok(())` if the message was written successfully
/// * `Err(PeerError::IoError)` on write failure
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```no_run
/// use tokio::net::TcpStream;
/// use literate_bittorrent::client::write_message;
/// use literate_bittorrent::messages::Message;
///
/// async fn example(stream: &mut TcpStream) {
///     // Send an interested message
///     write_message(stream, &Message::Interested).await.unwrap();
///
///     // Send a keep-alive
///     write_message(stream, &Message::KeepAlive).await.unwrap();
/// }
/// ```
pub async fn write_message(stream: &mut TcpStream, msg: &Message) -> Result<(), PeerError> {
    let bytes = msg.to_bytes();
    stream.write_all(&bytes).await?;
    Ok(())
}
@

The [[write_all]] method ensures atomic delivery---it will keep writing until
all bytes are sent or an error occurs. This is important because TCP is a stream
protocol and a single [[write]] call might not send all bytes if the kernel's
send buffer is full.

\subsubsection{Keep-Alive Scheduling}

To send keep-alives at the right time, we track when we last sent any message.
If too much time has passed since the last send, we send a keep-alive. The
[[send_keep_alive_if_needed]] function encapsulates this logic:

<<keep-alive scheduling>>=
/// Sends a keep-alive if needed based on the last send time.
///
/// Returns the new last send time (now) if keep-alive was sent,
/// otherwise returns the unchanged last_send_time.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `stream` - The TCP stream to write to
/// * `last_send_time` - When we last sent any message
///
/// # Returns
///
/// The updated last_send_time (either unchanged or set to now if keep-alive sent).
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns `PeerError::IoError` on write failure.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```no_run
/// use std::time::Instant;
/// use tokio::net::TcpStream;
/// use literate_bittorrent::client::send_keep_alive_if_needed;
///
/// async fn peer_loop(stream: &mut TcpStream) {
///     let mut last_send_time = Instant::now();
///
///     loop {
///         // ... process messages ...
///
///         // Check if we need to send a keep-alive
///         last_send_time = send_keep_alive_if_needed(stream, last_send_time)
///             .await
///             .expect("Failed to send keep-alive");
///
///         // ... wait for next event ...
///     }
/// }
/// ```
pub async fn send_keep_alive_if_needed(
    stream: &mut TcpStream,
    last_send_time: Instant,
) -> Result<Instant, PeerError> {
    if last_send_time.elapsed() >= Duration::from_secs(KEEP_ALIVE_INTERVAL_SECS) {
        write_message(stream, &Message::KeepAlive).await?;
        Ok(Instant::now())
    } else {
        Ok(last_send_time)
    }
}
@

The function returns the updated [[last_send_time]] so the caller can track
when the next keep-alive might be needed. The caller should update their
[[last_send_time]] variable whenever they send any message, not just keep-alives.

