\section{The Main Event Loop}

The event loop is the heart of the BitTorrent client. It multiplexes all async
operations using [[tokio::select!]], orchestrating peer messages, timers, and
shutdown signals into a coherent download process.

\subsection{Event Loop Design}

The event loop must handle several concurrent operations:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Peer Messages}: Read messages from all connected peers concurrently.
  When a message arrives, route it to the appropriate handler.

\item \textbf{Request Timeout}: Periodically check for timed-out block requests
  and reassign them to other peers.

\item \textbf{Re-Announce Timer}: Contact the tracker at the specified interval
  to refresh the peer list and report progress.

\item \textbf{Keep-Alive Timer}: Send keep-alive messages to peers that haven't
  received data recently, preventing connection timeouts.

\item \textbf{Shutdown Signal}: Check for graceful shutdown requests (Ctrl+C)
  and exit cleanly.
\end{enumerate}

The [[tokio::select!]] macro provides the mechanism to await multiple futures
concurrently, completing when any one of them is ready. This creates an event-driven
architecture where the loop responds to whichever event occurs first.

\subsection{Event Types}

We define an enum to represent the different events the loop handles:

<<event loop>>=
use tokio::time::interval;
use tracing::trace;

/// Request timeout check interval in seconds.
///
/// The event loop checks for timed-out requests every 30 seconds.
/// This is a balance between responsiveness (detecting timeouts quickly)
/// and overhead (not checking too frequently).
pub const REQUEST_TIMEOUT_CHECK_INTERVAL_SECS: u64 = 30;

/// Events that can occur during the event loop.
///
/// Each variant represents a distinct type of event that requires
/// handling in the main loop. The event loop uses `tokio::select!`
/// to await these events concurrently.
#[derive(Debug)]
pub enum EventLoopEvent {
    /// A message was received from a peer.
    ///
    /// Contains the peer's address and the message. The address is used
    /// to look up the connection state for message processing.
    PeerMessage {
        addr: SocketAddr,
        message: Result<Message, PeerError>,
    },

    /// A peer connection was lost or errored.
    ///
    /// The connection should be removed and replaced from the peer pool.
    PeerDisconnected {
        addr: SocketAddr,
        reason: String,
    },

    /// Time to check for timed-out block requests.
    ///
    /// Triggered every REQUEST_TIMEOUT_CHECK_INTERVAL_SECS seconds.
    RequestTimeoutCheck,

    /// Time to re-announce to the tracker.
    ///
    /// Triggered at the interval specified by the tracker response.
    ReAnnounce,

    /// Time to send keep-alives to idle peers.
    ///
    /// Triggered every KEEP_ALIVE_INTERVAL_SECS seconds.
    KeepAliveCheck,

    /// Shutdown was requested (Ctrl+C or programmatic).
    Shutdown,

    /// Download is complete (all pieces verified).
    DownloadComplete,
}

/// Result of processing one event loop iteration.
///
/// Guides the main loop on what action to take next.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum EventLoopAction {
    /// Continue processing events.
    Continue,

    /// Exit the loop due to shutdown request.
    ShutdownRequested,

    /// Exit the loop because download is complete.
    Complete,

    /// Exit the loop due to an error.
    Error(String),
}
@

The event types capture the essential information needed to process each kind
of occurrence. The [[EventLoopAction]] enum tells the main loop whether to
continue or exit, and why.

\subsection{Event Loop Context}

The event loop needs access to various pieces of state. Rather than passing
many parameters, we group them into a context struct that can be passed around:

<<event loop>>=
/// Context for the event loop, holding references to all required state.
///
/// This struct groups together the state needed by the event loop, avoiding
/// long parameter lists and making the code more readable. The context
/// borrows state rather than owning it, so the event loop doesn't take
/// ownership of client resources.
pub struct EventLoopContext<'a> {
    /// Manages peer connections (add/remove/iterate).
    pub connection_manager: &'a mut PeerConnectionManager,

    /// Tracks piece download state and verification.
    pub piece_manager: &'a mut PieceManager,

    /// Tracks which pieces each peer has (for rarest-first).
    pub availability: &'a mut PieceAvailability,

    /// Signals for shutdown coordination.
    pub shutdown: &'a ShutdownController,

    /// Pending request queues per peer (keyed by address).
    ///
    /// Separated from PeerConnection to allow concurrent iteration.
    pub request_queues: &'a mut HashMap<SocketAddr, PeerRequestQueue>,

    /// In-progress piece buffers (keyed by piece index).
    ///
    /// Contains the partially-downloaded data for each piece.
    pub in_progress_pieces: &'a mut HashMap<u32, InProgressPiece>,

    /// Expected piece hashes for verification.
    pub piece_hashes: &'a [[u8; 20]],

    /// Number of pieces in this torrent.
    pub num_pieces: u32,

    /// Our peer ID for block requests.
    pub our_peer_id: [u8; 20],

    /// Tracker re-announce interval in seconds.
    pub tracker_interval: u64,

    /// Flag indicating download is complete.
    pub download_complete: &'a mut bool,
}
@

\subsection{Reading from Peers}

Reading from peers in the event loop presents a challenge: we need to read
from any peer that has data available, but we can't hold mutable borrows of
all streams simultaneously. Instead, we poll peers round-robin within the
select! loop, reading from whichever has data available first.

Since tokio::select! already provides fair polling among its branches, and
peer reading is just one branch, we can use a simpler approach: try to read
from the first available peer connection.

<<event loop>>=
/// Reads a message from the first available peer connection.
///
/// This function tries to read from a single peer. The event loop calls
/// this repeatedly within select! to achieve fair multiplexing across
/// all ready events.
///
/// # Returns
///
/// - `Some((addr, result))` if we attempted to read from a peer
/// - `None` if there are no connections
async fn read_from_first_peer(
    connections: &mut HashMap<SocketAddr, PeerConnection>,
) -> Option<(SocketAddr, Result<Message, PeerError>)> {
    // Get first connection (HashMap iteration order is arbitrary but stable)
    let addr = *connections.keys().next()?;

    let conn = connections.get_mut(&addr)?;
    let result = read_message(&mut conn.stream).await;
    Some((addr, result))
}
@

\subsection{The Main Event Loop}

The main event loop ties everything together. It uses [[tokio::select!]] to
multiplex all event sources, processing whichever event occurs first.

<<event loop>>=
/// Runs the main event loop for the BitTorrent client.
///
/// This is the heart of the client, orchestrating all async operations:
/// - Reading messages from connected peers
/// - Checking for request timeouts
/// - Re-announcing to the tracker
/// - Sending keep-alive messages
/// - Handling graceful shutdown
///
/// The loop continues until:
/// - Shutdown is requested (returns `ShutdownRequested`)
/// - Download completes (returns `Complete`)
/// - An unrecoverable error occurs (returns `Error`)
///
/// # Event Priority
///
/// When multiple events are ready simultaneously, `tokio::select!` picks
/// one pseudo-randomly. This is fine for our use case since all events
/// need to be processed eventually.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `ctx` - Mutable reference to the event loop context containing all state
///
/// # Returns
///
/// `EventLoopAction` indicating why the loop exited.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// let action = run_event_loop(&mut ctx).await;
/// match action {
///     EventLoopAction::Complete => println!("Download finished!"),
///     EventLoopAction::ShutdownRequested => println!("Shutdown requested"),
///     EventLoopAction::Error(e) => eprintln!("Error: {}", e),
///     EventLoopAction::Continue => unreachable!("Loop should not return Continue"),
/// }
/// ```
pub async fn run_event_loop(ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>) -> EventLoopAction {
    // Initialize timers
    let mut request_timeout_interval = interval(
        Duration::from_secs(REQUEST_TIMEOUT_CHECK_INTERVAL_SECS)
    );
    let mut keep_alive_interval = interval(
        Duration::from_secs(KEEP_ALIVE_INTERVAL_SECS)
    );
    let mut re_announce_interval = interval(
        Duration::from_secs(ctx.tracker_interval)
    );

    // Skip the first tick (intervals fire immediately on creation)
    request_timeout_interval.tick().await;
    keep_alive_interval.tick().await;
    re_announce_interval.tick().await;

    let mut iteration: u64 = 0;

    loop {
        iteration += 1;
        trace!(iteration, "event loop iteration start");

        // Check for shutdown first (non-blocking)
        if ctx.shutdown.is_shutdown_requested() {
            info!("shutdown requested, exiting event loop");
            return EventLoopAction::ShutdownRequested;
        }

        // Check for completion
        if *ctx.download_complete {
            info!("download complete, exiting event loop");
            return EventLoopAction::Complete;
        }

        // Wait for the next event
        let event = tokio::select! {
            biased;

            // Shutdown check has highest priority
            _ = std::future::ready(()), if ctx.shutdown.is_shutdown_requested() => {
                EventLoopEvent::Shutdown
            }

            // Request timeout check
            _ = request_timeout_interval.tick() => {
                EventLoopEvent::RequestTimeoutCheck
            }

            // Keep-alive check
            _ = keep_alive_interval.tick() => {
                EventLoopEvent::KeepAliveCheck
            }

            // Re-announce timer
            _ = re_announce_interval.tick() => {
                EventLoopEvent::ReAnnounce
            }

            // Read from peers (only if we have connections)
            Some((addr, result)) = read_from_first_peer_wrapper(
                ctx.connection_manager
            ) => {
                match result {
                    Ok(msg) => EventLoopEvent::PeerMessage { addr, message: Ok(msg) },
                    Err(PeerError::ConnectionClosed) => {
                        EventLoopEvent::PeerDisconnected {
                            addr,
                            reason: "connection closed".to_string(),
                        }
                    }
                    Err(e) => {
                        EventLoopEvent::PeerDisconnected {
                            addr,
                            reason: e.to_string(),
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        };

        trace!(?event, "processing event");

        // Process the event
        let action = handle_event(event, ctx).await;

        match action {
            EventLoopAction::Continue => continue,
            other => return other,
        }
    }
}

/// Wrapper to read from peers through the connection manager.
///
/// This function provides access to the internal connections HashMap
/// through the PeerConnectionManager interface.
async fn read_from_first_peer_wrapper(
    manager: &mut PeerConnectionManager,
) -> Option<(SocketAddr, Result<Message, PeerError>)> {
    // Get the connections as a mutable reference
    let connections = manager.connections_map_mut();
    read_from_first_peer(connections).await
}
@

The [[biased]] keyword in [[tokio::select!]] ensures that branches are checked
in order when multiple are ready. This gives shutdown the highest priority,
ensuring responsive termination.

\subsection{Event Handling}

Each event type has a corresponding handler that processes it and returns
an action for the main loop:

<<event loop>>=
/// Handles a single event from the event loop.
///
/// Dispatches to the appropriate handler based on event type and
/// returns an action indicating what the main loop should do next.
async fn handle_event(
    event: EventLoopEvent,
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) -> EventLoopAction {
    match event {
        EventLoopEvent::Shutdown => {
            info!("received shutdown event");
            EventLoopAction::ShutdownRequested
        }

        EventLoopEvent::DownloadComplete => {
            info!("download complete event");
            EventLoopAction::Complete
        }

        EventLoopEvent::PeerMessage { addr, message } => {
            handle_peer_message(addr, message, ctx).await
        }

        EventLoopEvent::PeerDisconnected { addr, reason } => {
            handle_peer_disconnected(addr, &reason, ctx).await
        }

        EventLoopEvent::RequestTimeoutCheck => {
            handle_request_timeouts(ctx).await
        }

        EventLoopEvent::KeepAliveCheck => {
            handle_keep_alives(ctx).await
        }

        EventLoopEvent::ReAnnounce => {
            // Re-announce is handled externally (tracker module)
            // We just signal that it's time
            debug!("re-announce timer fired");
            EventLoopAction::Continue
        }
    }
}
@

\subsubsection{Peer Message Handling}

When we receive a message from a peer, we route it to the appropriate handler
based on the message type:

<<event loop>>=
/// Handles an incoming message from a peer.
///
/// Routes the message to the appropriate handler and takes any
/// necessary follow-up actions (like requesting more blocks).
async fn handle_peer_message(
    addr: SocketAddr,
    message: Result<Message, PeerError>,
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) -> EventLoopAction {
    let msg = match message {
        Ok(m) => m,
        Err(e) => {
            debug!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "error receiving message");
            return handle_peer_disconnected(addr, &e.to_string(), ctx).await;
        }
    };

    // Get the connection (if it still exists)
    let conn = match ctx.connection_manager.get_connection_mut(&addr) {
        Some(c) => c,
        None => {
            debug!(peer = %addr, "received message from unknown peer");
            return EventLoopAction::Continue;
        }
    };

    // Update last receive time
    conn.mark_received();

    match msg {
        Message::KeepAlive => {
            trace!(peer = %addr, "received keep-alive");
        }

        Message::Choke => {
            let cancelled = handle_incoming_choke(conn);
            // Log cancelled requests - they will be available for re-request
            for req in cancelled {
                trace!(
                    piece = req.piece_index,
                    offset = req.offset,
                    "cancelled request returned to pool"
                );
            }
        }

        Message::Unchoke => {
            handle_incoming_unchoke(conn);
            // Trigger block requests now that we're unchoked
            trigger_block_requests_for_peer(addr, ctx).await;
        }

        Message::Interested => {
            conn.state.handle_interested();
            debug!(peer = %addr, "peer is interested in our pieces");
        }

        Message::NotInterested => {
            conn.state.handle_not_interested();
            debug!(peer = %addr, "peer is not interested in our pieces");
        }

        Message::Have(piece_index) => {
            let should_be_interested = handle_incoming_have(
                &mut conn.state,
                piece_index,
                ctx.availability,
                ctx.piece_manager,
            );

            if should_be_interested && !conn.state.am_interested {
                conn.state.am_interested = true;
                // Send Interested message
                if let Err(e) = write_message(&mut conn.stream, &Message::Interested).await {
                    debug!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "failed to send interested");
                } else {
                    conn.mark_sent();
                    debug!(peer = %addr, "sent interested after have");
                }
            }
        }

        Message::Bitfield(bits) => {
            match handle_incoming_bitfield(
                &mut conn.state,
                &bits,
                ctx.availability,
                ctx.piece_manager,
            ) {
                Ok(should_be_interested) => {
                    if should_be_interested && !conn.state.am_interested {
                        conn.state.am_interested = true;
                        if let Err(e) = write_message(&mut conn.stream, &Message::Interested).await {
                            debug!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "failed to send interested");
                        } else {
                            conn.mark_sent();
                            debug!(peer = %addr, "sent interested after bitfield");
                        }
                    }
                }
                Err(e) => {
                    // Invalid bitfield is a protocol error - keep at warn level
                    warn!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "invalid bitfield");
                    return handle_peer_disconnected(addr, &e.to_string(), ctx).await;
                }
            }
        }

        Message::Request { index, begin, length } => {
            // We don't serve requests (download-only client)
            debug!(
                peer = %addr,
                piece = index,
                offset = begin,
                len = length,
                "ignoring request (download-only client)"
            );
        }

        Message::Piece { index, begin, data } => {
            // Get or create in-progress piece
            let in_progress = ctx.in_progress_pieces.entry(index).or_insert_with(|| {
                let piece_size = ctx.piece_manager
                    .get_piece_size(index)
                    .unwrap_or(0);
                InProgressPiece::new(index, piece_size)
            });

            let expected_hash = ctx.piece_hashes.get(index as usize)
                .unwrap_or(&[0u8; 20]);

            // Need to get connection again due to borrow checker
            let conn = match ctx.connection_manager.get_connection_mut(&addr) {
                Some(c) => c,
                None => {
                    warn!(peer = %addr, "connection disappeared during piece handling");
                    return EventLoopAction::Continue;
                }
            };

            let result = handle_incoming_piece(
                conn,
                index,
                begin,
                &data,
                in_progress,
                expected_hash,
            );

            match result {
                PieceHandleResult::BlockStored => {
                    // Continue downloading - request more blocks if queue has space
                    trigger_block_requests_for_peer(addr, ctx).await;
                }
                PieceHandleResult::PieceVerified(piece_idx) => {
                    info!(piece = piece_idx, "piece verified!");

                    // Mark piece as verified in piece manager
                    if let Err(e) = ctx.piece_manager.mark_verified(piece_idx as u32) {
                        warn!(piece = piece_idx, error = %e, "failed to mark piece verified");
                    }

                    // Remove from in-progress
                    ctx.in_progress_pieces.remove(&(piece_idx as u32));

                    // Check if download is complete
                    if ctx.piece_manager.is_complete() {
                        *ctx.download_complete = true;
                        return EventLoopAction::Complete;
                    }

                    // Trigger requests for next pieces
                    trigger_block_requests_all_peers(ctx).await;
                }
                PieceHandleResult::PieceHashFailed(piece_idx) => {
                    warn!(piece = piece_idx, "piece hash verification failed, will retry");

                    // Mark the piece as failed so it can be re-downloaded
                    ctx.in_progress_pieces.remove(&(piece_idx as u32));
                    if let Err(e) = ctx.piece_manager.mark_failed(piece_idx as u32) {
                        warn!(piece = piece_idx, error = %e, "failed to mark piece as failed");
                    }

                    // Trigger requests to try again
                    trigger_block_requests_all_peers(ctx).await;
                }
                PieceHandleResult::UnexpectedBlock => {
                    // Ignore, already logged in handler
                }
            }
        }

        Message::Cancel { index, begin, length } => {
            // We don't serve requests, so we don't track cancellations
            trace!(
                peer = %addr,
                piece = index,
                offset = begin,
                len = length,
                "received cancel (ignored - download-only)"
            );
        }
    }

    EventLoopAction::Continue
}
@

\subsubsection{Peer Disconnection}

When a peer disconnects, we remove them from the connection pool and try to
replace them from the available peer queue:

<<event loop>>=
/// Handles peer disconnection.
///
/// Removes the peer from active connections and attempts to replace them
/// from the available peer pool.
async fn handle_peer_disconnected(
    addr: SocketAddr,
    reason: &str,
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) -> EventLoopAction {
    info!(peer = %addr, reason = %reason, "peer disconnected");

    // Remove from connection manager
    if let Some(conn) = ctx.connection_manager.remove_connection(&addr) {
        // Update availability - remove peer's pieces from global tracking
        ctx.availability.remove_peer_bitfield(&conn.state.bitfield);
    }

    // Remove the peer's request queue
    ctx.request_queues.remove(&addr);

    // Try to replace with a new peer
    // (Connection establishment is handled elsewhere, we just note we need more)
    if ctx.connection_manager.needs_more_connections() {
        debug!(
            active = ctx.connection_manager.active_count(),
            available = ctx.connection_manager.available_count(),
            "peer disconnected, should connect to more peers"
        );
    }

    EventLoopAction::Continue
}
@

\subsubsection{Request Timeout Handling}

Periodically, we check for timed-out block requests and reassign them:

<<event loop>>=
/// Handles the request timeout check timer.
///
/// Iterates through all peer request queues, finds timed-out requests,
/// and attempts to reassign them to other peers.
async fn handle_request_timeouts(
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) -> EventLoopAction {
    trace!("checking for request timeouts");

    // First pass: collect all timed-out requests with their peer addresses
    // This avoids borrowing issues by not holding mutable references across iterations
    let mut timed_out_requests: Vec<(SocketAddr, BlockRequest)> = Vec::new();

    let peer_addrs: Vec<SocketAddr> = ctx.request_queues.keys().copied().collect();

    for addr in peer_addrs {
        if let Some(queue) = ctx.request_queues.get(&addr) {
            for timestamped in queue.check_timeouts() {
                timed_out_requests.push((addr, timestamped.request.clone()));
            }
        }
    }

    // Second pass: process each timed-out request
    for (addr, request) in timed_out_requests {
        debug!(
            peer = %addr,
            piece = request.piece_index,
            offset = request.offset,
            "request timed out"
        );

        // Remove from the original peer's queue
        if let Some(queue) = ctx.request_queues.get_mut(&addr) {
            queue.remove(request.piece_index, request.offset);
        }

        // Create cancel message
        let cancel = Message::Cancel {
            index: request.piece_index,
            begin: request.offset,
            length: request.length,
        };

        // Send cancel to original peer
        if let Some(conn) = ctx.connection_manager.get_connection_mut(&addr) {
            if let Err(e) = write_message(&mut conn.stream, &cancel).await {
                debug!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "failed to send cancel");
            } else {
                conn.mark_sent();
            }
        }

        // Find an alternative peer that has the piece and isn't choking us
        let alt_addr = ctx.connection_manager
            .connections()
            .filter(|(peer_addr, _)| **peer_addr != addr)
            .filter(|(_, conn)| conn.state.has_piece(request.piece_index))
            .filter(|(_, conn)| !conn.state.peer_choking)
            .map(|(peer_addr, _)| *peer_addr)
            .next();

        // Re-request from alternative if available
        if let Some(alt_addr) = alt_addr {
            debug!(
                piece = request.piece_index,
                offset = request.offset,
                new_peer = %alt_addr,
                "reassigning timed-out request"
            );

            // Queue the request to the alternative peer
            if let Some(alt_queue) = ctx.request_queues.get_mut(&alt_addr) {
                let new_request = BlockRequest::new(
                    request.piece_index,
                    request.offset,
                    request.length,
                    ctx.our_peer_id,
                );
                alt_queue.push(new_request.clone());

                // Send the request message
                if let Some(alt_conn) = ctx.connection_manager.get_connection_mut(&alt_addr) {
                    let msg = Message::Request {
                        index: new_request.piece_index,
                        begin: new_request.offset,
                        length: new_request.length,
                    };
                    if let Err(e) = write_message(&mut alt_conn.stream, &msg).await {
                        debug!(peer = %alt_addr, error = %e, "failed to send request");
                    } else {
                        alt_conn.mark_sent();
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    EventLoopAction::Continue
}
@

\subsubsection{Keep-Alive Handling}

Send keep-alives to peers that haven't received data recently:

<<event loop>>=
/// Handles the keep-alive check timer.
///
/// Iterates through all peer connections and sends keep-alives to those
/// that haven't had any messages sent recently.
async fn handle_keep_alives(
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) -> EventLoopAction {
    trace!("checking for keep-alives");

    let keep_alive_threshold = Duration::from_secs(KEEP_ALIVE_INTERVAL_SECS);

    // Collect addresses to avoid borrowing issues
    let peer_addrs: Vec<SocketAddr> = ctx.connection_manager
        .connections()
        .map(|(addr, _)| *addr)
        .collect();

    for addr in peer_addrs {
        if let Some(conn) = ctx.connection_manager.get_connection_mut(&addr) {
            if conn.last_send_time.elapsed() >= keep_alive_threshold {
                match write_message(&mut conn.stream, &Message::KeepAlive).await {
                    Ok(()) => {
                        conn.mark_sent();
                        trace!(peer = %addr, "sent keep-alive");
                    }
                    Err(e) => {
                        debug!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "failed to send keep-alive");
                        // Don't disconnect here - let the next read fail
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    EventLoopAction::Continue
}
@

\subsubsection{Triggering Block Requests}

After receiving an unchoke or completing a piece, we need to request more blocks:

<<event loop>>=
/// Triggers block requests for a specific peer.
///
/// Called after receiving an unchoke or when queue space becomes available.
async fn trigger_block_requests_for_peer(
    addr: SocketAddr,
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) {
    let conn = match ctx.connection_manager.get_connection_mut(&addr) {
        Some(c) => c,
        None => {
            debug!(peer = %addr, "connection gone, skipping block requests");
            return;
        }
    };

    // Can't request if peer is choking us
    if conn.state.peer_choking {
        return;
    }

    // Get or create request queue for this peer
    let queue = ctx.request_queues.entry(addr).or_insert_with(|| {
        PeerRequestQueue::new(DEFAULT_MAX_PIPELINE_LENGTH)
    });

    // Fill the request queue
    let requests = fill_request_queue(
        &conn.state,
        queue,
        ctx.piece_manager,
        ctx.availability,
        ctx.our_peer_id,
    );

    // Send the request messages
    for request in requests {
        let msg = Message::Request {
            index: request.piece_index,
            begin: request.offset,
            length: request.length,
        };

        // Get connection again (borrow checker)
        if let Some(conn) = ctx.connection_manager.get_connection_mut(&addr) {
            if let Err(e) = write_message(&mut conn.stream, &msg).await {
                debug!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "failed to send request");
                break;
            }
            conn.mark_sent();
            trace!(
                peer = %addr,
                piece = request.piece_index,
                offset = request.offset,
                "sent block request"
            );
        }
    }
}

/// Triggers block requests for all unchoked peers.
///
/// Called after completing a piece to distribute requests across peers.
async fn trigger_block_requests_all_peers(
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) {
    // Collect addresses to avoid borrowing issues
    let peer_addrs: Vec<SocketAddr> = ctx.connection_manager
        .connections()
        .filter(|(_, conn)| !conn.state.peer_choking)
        .map(|(addr, _)| *addr)
        .collect();

    for addr in peer_addrs {
        trigger_block_requests_for_peer(addr, ctx).await;
    }
}
@

\subsection{Connection Manager Extension}

We need to add a method to [[PeerConnectionManager]] to access the internal
connections map for the event loop:

<<event loop connection manager extension>>=
impl PeerConnectionManager {
    /// Returns a mutable reference to the internal connections map.
    ///
    /// This is used by the event loop to read from multiple peer streams
    /// concurrently. The method is intentionally not public in the normal
    /// API since direct map access bypasses the manager's invariants.
    pub(crate) fn connections_map_mut(&mut self) -> &mut HashMap<SocketAddr, PeerConnection> {
        &mut self.connections
    }
}
